From 0825d742c70d1b35b5643fd8f6042bf2da0d687b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jky72 <jky72@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 11:53:43 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] perf(shader): render the background into a scaled target and
 reuse it

The background shader is a full-screen procedural scene, so integrated GPUs shade every pixel
of it. It now renders into a texture sized by an integer divisor (4K -> 1920x1080), which keeps
the authored pixel grid and dither intact and lets the compositor upscale with a plain texel
fetch while the terminal's own texture stays at native resolution - text is never downscaled.
The transpiled shader multiplies gl_FragCoord by the upscale factor so its math still believes
it runs at native size, and readability dimming moved to the native compositor.

On top of that the scaled target is reused: the artwork is redrawn 30 times a second while
animating and once a second when animation is off, because a cached background costs nothing to
show. A fresh target forces an immediate redraw, and the un-scaled path (discrete GPUs) still
draws straight into the output view, so nothing changes there.

Measured on the AMD integrated GPU (open terminal, 2360x1412, low_power, focused window):
97.0% -> 51.4% 3D engine busy, and 13.5% with animation off. App baseline without any shader is
6.6%, so the shader's own share fell from ~90% to ~38%. The pixel-count model predicted more; the
remaining cost clearly does not scale with the render target, so the next step is profiling
rather than more resolution reduction. See docs/2026-09-19-2d-optimization-techniques.md in the
deploy repository for the technique survey and the numbers.
